    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-16727",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/655a47bf250df8e0694d8a82d5b22efde626681684fb78042767380ff202685e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{2}f(x-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{1}{2}f(x-1),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/bdb1b3bc6704af4deab77e027c5dd8f00eb929d37ddf1765866ff9d6cdbc5a7e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/ce42d16b09528a1a5ef0b2e0866081af8e5593ebdacb741be620944fd6a7dcd0.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{2}f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/640e2dd9fc8caea9d0a2b03bfdb35ad25f656e40e409e7e9778745fe16c595d9.png)",
